The code statements that lead to the bug are:
DEFINE_FLEX(struct hci_cp_le_big_create_sync, cp, bis, num_bis, 0x11);
memcpy(cp->bis, conn->bis, conn->num_bis);
Reason: The `DEFINE_FLEX` macro allocates a stack buffer with space for only 0x11 (17) BIS entries, but `conn->num_bis` can be at most `HCI_MAX_ISO_BIS` (31). The `memcpy` writes `conn->num_bis` bytes into the buffer, which can overflow the allocated stack memory when `conn->num_bis` exceeds 17, corrupting adjacent stack data.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
DEFINE_FLEX(struct hci_cp_le_big_create_sync, cp, bis, num_bis,
		    HCI_MAX_ISO_BIS);
Reason: Replacing the hardcoded `0x11` with `HCI_MAX_ISO_BIS` ensures the allocated stack buffer has sufficient capacity to hold the maximum number of BIS entries that `conn->bis` can actually contain, preventing the stack buffer overflow.

